/*
------------------------------------------------------------
Problem    : Find K Pairs with Smallest Sums
Platform   : LeetCode
Topic      : Heap / Priority Queue
Difficulty : Medium

Problem:
You are given two integer arrays nums1 and nums2 sorted in 
non-decreasing order and an integer k.

Define a pair (u, v) which consists of one element from the 
first array and one element from the second array.

Return the k pairs (u1, v1), (u2, v2), ..., (uk, vk) with 
the smallest sums.

Approach:
Since both arrays are sorted, the smallest possible sum is 
always nums1[0] + nums2[0]. The next smallest sum must involve 
either the next element in nums1 or the next element in nums2.

This structure perfectly fits a Min-Heap (Priority Queue).
We initialize the heap with the first element of nums2 paired 
with the first min(k, nums1.size()) elements of nums1.

Each entry in the heap stores: {sum, index_in_nums1, index_in_nums2}.

In each step, we extract the minimum sum pair, add it to our 
result, and then push the next pair from the same nums1 element 
but the next nums2 element (i.e., index_in_nums2 + 1).

Why do we use a Min-Heap?
Because we need to repeatedly find and extract the smallest sum 
among a set of candidate pairs. A Min-Heap allows us to do this 
efficiently in O(log K) time per extraction, avoiding the need 
to generate and sort all possible N * M pairs.

Example:
nums1 = [1, 7, 11], nums2 = [2, 4, 6], k = 3

Step 1: Initialize Min-Heap
Push pairs with nums2[0]:
    (1+2, i=0, j=0) -> sum = 3
    (7+2, i=1, j=0) -> sum = 9
    (11+2, i=2, j=0) -> sum = 13

Step 2: Extract k times
k = 3:
    Pop (3, 0, 0). Result: [[1, 2]].
    Push next from nums2: (1+4, 0, 1) -> sum = 5.
    Heap: (5, 0, 1), (9, 1, 0), (13, 2, 0)

k = 2:
    Pop (5, 0, 1). Result: [[1, 2], [1, 4]].
    Push next from nums2: (1+6, 0, 2) -> sum = 7.
    Heap: (7, 0, 2), (9, 1, 0), (13, 2, 0)

k = 1:
    Pop (7, 0, 2). Result: [[1, 2], [1, 4], [1, 6]].
    j+1 is out of bounds, do not push.
    Heap: (9, 1, 0), (13, 2, 0)

k = 0: Stop.

Result: [[1, 2], [1, 4], [1, 6]]

------------------------------------------------------------
Dry Run: nums1 = [1, 1, 2], nums2 = [1, 2, 3], k = 2
------------------------------------------------------------

Step 1: Initialize Min-Heap
Push pairs with nums2[0]:
    (1+1, i=0, j=0) -> sum = 2
    (1+1, i=1, j=0) -> sum = 2
    (2+1, i=2, j=0) -> sum = 3

Step 2: Extract k times
k = 2:
    Pop (2, 0, 0). Result: [[1, 1]].
    Push next from nums2: (1+2, 0, 1) -> sum = 3.
    Heap: (2, 1, 0), (3, 0, 1), (3, 2, 0)

k = 1:
    Pop (2, 1, 0). Result: [[1, 1], [1, 1]].
    Push next from nums2: (1+2, 1, 1) -> sum = 3.
    Heap: (3, 0, 1), (3, 1, 1), (3, 2, 0)

k = 0: Stop.

Result: [[1, 1], [1, 1]]

------------------------------------------------------------
Why not generate all pairs and sort?
------------------------------------------------------------

Generating all pairs would take O(N * M) time and space, which 
is highly inefficient and will result in Memory Limit Exceeded 
(MLE) or Time Limit Exceeded (TLE) for large arrays.

By using a Min-Heap, we only keep at most K elements in the 
heap at any time, reducing the space complexity to O(K) and 
the time complexity to O(K log K).

------------------------------------------------------------
Algorithm
------------------------------------------------------------

1. Edge Case:
       If nums1 or nums2 is empty, or k == 0, return empty list.

2. Initialize Min-Heap:
       Store tuples of {sum, i, j}.
       For i from 0 to min(k, nums1.size()) - 1:
           push {nums1[i] + nums2[0], i, 0}

3. Extract K Pairs:
       While k > 0 and heap is not empty:
           Pop {sum, i, j} from heap.
           Add {nums1[i], nums2[j]} to result.
           
           If j + 1 < nums2.size():
               push {nums1[i] + nums2[j+1], i, j+1} to heap.
           
           k--

4. Return result.

------------------------------------------------------------
Complexity
------------------------------------------------------------

Time Complexity:
    O(K log K)
    In the worst case, we perform K extractions and K insertions 
    into the Min-Heap. Each heap operation takes O(log K) time 
    since the heap size is at most K.

Space Complexity:
    O(K)
    The Min-Heap will store at most K elements at any given time.
    (Excluding the space required for the output array).

------------------------------------------------------------
*/

class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> kSmallestPairs(vector<int>& nums1, vector<int>& nums2, int k) {
        vector<vector<int>> ans;

        // Edge case: if either array is empty or k is 0
        if(nums1.empty() || nums2.empty() || k == 0){
            return ans;
        }

        // Min-Heap to store {sum, index_in_nums1, index_in_nums2}
        // We use greater<> to make it a min-heap instead of the default max-heap
        priority_queue<
            tuple<int, int, int>,
            vector<tuple<int, int, int>>,
            greater<tuple<int, int, int>>
        > minheap;

        // Initialize the heap with the first element of nums2 
        // paired with the first min(k, nums1.size()) elements of nums1
        for(int i = 0; i < nums1.size() && i < k; i++){
            minheap.push({nums1[i] + nums2[0], i, 0});
        }

        // Extract the smallest sum k times
        while(k-- && !minheap.empty()){
            auto [sum, i, j] = minheap.top();
            minheap.pop();
            
            // Add the pair to our result
            ans.push_back({nums1[i], nums2[j]});

            // If there is a next element in nums2, push it to the heap
            if(j + 1 < nums2.size()){
                minheap.push({nums1[i] + nums2[j + 1], i, j + 1});
            }
        }
        
        return ans;
    }
};
